There's no `.csproj`, so I can't confirm the target framework. Here's the lightest path depending on your .NET version:

### .NET 8+ → Use the built-in `TimeProvider`

`TimeProvider` is already in the BCL — no new interfaces needed. Inject it via a constructor parameter with a default:

```csharp
public class InvoiceCalculator
{
    private readonly TimeProvider _time;

    public InvoiceCalculator(TimeProvider? timeProvider = null)
        => _time = timeProvider ?? TimeProvider.System;

    public bool IsOverdue(DateTimeOffset dueDate)
        => _time.GetUtcNow() > dueDate;
}
```

In tests, use `Microsoft.Extensions.Time.Testing.FakeTimeProvider`:

```csharp
var fake = new FakeTimeProvider(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 15, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
var calc = new InvoiceCalculator(fake);

Assert.True(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
```

(`FakeTimeProvider` is in the `Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing` NuGet package.)

### Pre-.NET 8 → Thin `IClock` interface

Define a one-method interface and inject it the same way.

